# Line endings. These files are consumed by Linux tooling -- psql, bash, the
# self-hosted runner -- and must stay LF in the working tree even when checked
# out on Windows with core.autocrlf=true. This is not cosmetic:
#
#   * A CRLF .sql file changes the *contents* of a dollar-quoted literal. The
#     pgTAP suite supabase/tests/003_data_api_grants.test.sql compares a
#     multi-line expected string against `string_agg(..., e'\n')`; with CRLF
#     the literal carries \r and the aggregate does not, so two assertions
#     failed on Windows while passing in CI, printing a have/want diff whose
#     two sides look identical.
#   * A CRLF file with a shebang fails on Linux with a bad-interpreter error,
#     because the \r becomes part of the interpreter path. That covers the .sh
#     scripts -- scripts/provision-*.sh, .claude/hooks/session-start.sh,
#     scripts/check-deploy-secrets.sh -- and also scripts/verify-supabase-sql.mjs,
#     which is mode 755 and starts `#!/usr/bin/env node`. The .mjs case was
#     missed until issue #138: the rationale above was written about executable
#     scripts, but the patterns only listed the two extensions, so the one
#     executable script that is neither .sh nor .sql was pinned by nothing.
#
# `.mjs` is pinned by extension rather than only where a shebang exists today,
# to match how the two patterns above already work: the next executable .mjs
# then needs no change here. tests/foundation/ci-configuration-contract.test.ts
# asserts the coverage mechanically -- it takes the mode bits from
# `git ls-files -s` and the resolved attribute from `git check-attr`, so a new
# executable script with a shebang and no matching pattern fails the suite.
#
# The committed blobs are already LF; this only pins what lands on disk.
*.sql text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf
